{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    This uses a dynamic programming approach with O(n^2) time and space complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of substring s[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # Every single character is a palindrome of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the table for lengths from 2 to n.\n    # 'i' iterates from the end of the string backwards to the beginning.\n    # 'j' iterates from 'i + 1' to the end of the string.\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                # If characters match, they can form a palindrome.\n                # Add 2 to the LPS of the inner substring s[i+1...j-1].\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + (dp[i+1][j-1] if i+1 <= j-1 else 0)\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take the maximum of excluding s[i] or s[j].\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1])\n\n    return dp[0][n-1]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/252", "solution": "import math\ndef convert(value):\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a complex number or real number to polar coordinates (radius, angle).\n    Handles int, float, and complex inputs, returning (r, theta) where theta is in radians.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Normalize input 'value' to a complex number to extract real and imaginary parts.\n    # This implicitly handles int, float, and complex types.\n    c = complex(value)\n    x = c.real\n    y = c.imag\n\n    # Define epsilon for floating-point comparisons, as per design context.\n    EPSILON = 1e-12\n\n    # Calculate radius (r) using Euclidean distance.\n    r = math.hypot(x, y)\n\n    # Handle the special case where the radius is zero, as per business rules.\n    if r <= EPSILON:\n        r = 0.0  # Ensure radius is exactly 0.0 for near-zero inputs.\n        theta = 0.0  # Angle is 0.0 for (0,0), as per business decision.\n    else:\n        # Calculate angle (theta) using atan2 to preserve quadrant information.\n        theta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\n    return r, theta"}
