{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "def count_Primes_nums(num):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of prime numbers less than the given non-negative number using the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\n    Returns 0 for numbers less than or equal to 2, as 0 and 1 are not prime and 2 is not less than 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num <= 2:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize a boolean array 'sieve' where sieve[i] is True if i is potentially prime.\n    # Using bytearray for memory efficiency, where 1 means true and 0 means false.\n    sieve = bytearray(b'\\x01') * num\n    sieve[0:2] = b'\\x00\\x00'  # 0 and 1 are not prime\n\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of num.\n    # Numbers beyond sqrt(num) will have already had their multiples marked by smaller primes.\n    for p in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):\n        if sieve[p]:  # If p is prime\n            # Mark all multiples of p as not prime.\n            # Start marking from p*p, as smaller multiples (p*2, p*3, etc.)\n            # would have already been marked by primes smaller than p.\n            start_index = p * p\n            step = p\n            \n            # Efficiently mark multiples using slice assignment.\n            # Ensure start_index is within bounds before attempting to slice.\n            if start_index < num:\n                # Calculate the number of elements to be marked as not prime.\n                count_of_multiples = (num - 1 - start_index) // step + 1\n                sieve[start_index:num:step] = b'\\x00' * count_of_multiples\n    \n    # The sum of the sieve (where 1 represents a prime) gives the total count of primes.\n    return sum(sieve)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/255", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple, Any, Iterable\ndef combinations_colors(items: Iterable[Any], n: int) -> List[Tuple[Any, ...]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all combinations with repetition of elements from the input list for a given length n.\n    Returns a list of tuples, one for each combination.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return [()]\n\n    elements = list(items) # Ensure elements is a list for consistent indexing\n    k = len(elements)\n    \n    # If the input list is empty and n > 0, there are no combinations.\n    # The default logic handles this naturally (k=0 will lead to the loop breaking immediately).\n    if k == 0:\n        return []\n\n    indices = [0] * n\n    result = []\n\n    while True:\n        # Form the current combination from the elements using the current indices\n        current_combination = tuple(elements[i] for i in indices)\n        result.append(current_combination)\n\n        # Find the rightmost index that can be incremented\n        incremented = False\n        for pos in reversed(range(n)):\n            if indices[pos] + 1 < k:\n                indices[pos] += 1\n                # Reset all indices to the right of 'pos' to 0\n                for j in range(pos + 1, n):\n                    indices[j] = 0\n                incremented = True\n                break\n        \n        # If no index could be incremented, all combinations have been generated\n        if not incremented:\n            break\n\n    return result"}
